7592186287892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287892 #edb/ref 1071 "The coefficient of ${x}^{22}$ in the expansion of ${(3+{x}^{2})}^{13}$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2ca0dd6-55a4-5e14-9378-5d6846fa942b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287892 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3+{x}^{2})}^{13},$ we have $a=3,$ $b={x}^{2},$ and $n=13.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+{x}^{2})}^{13}=(\\frac{13}{0}){3}^{13}{({x}^{2})}^{0}+(\\frac{13}{1}){3}^{13-1}{({x}^{2})}^{1}+(\\frac{13}{2}){3}^{13-2}{({x}^{2})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{13}{13}){3}^{13-13}{({x}^{2})}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{22}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{13}{11}){3}^{13-11}{({x}^{2})}^{11}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{13}{11}){3}^{13-11}{({x}^{2})}^{11} & =78\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{22} \\\\  & =78\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{22} \\\\  & =702{x}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{22}$ is $702.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287893 #edb/ref 1071 "The coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ in the expansion of ${(2+x)}^{15}$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fe2343c-e168-571c-8f87-3172e7d9973d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287893 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+x)}^{15},$ we have $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+x)^{15}=(\\frac{15}{0}){2}^{15}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{15}{1}){2}^{15-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{15}{2}){2}^{15-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{15}{15}){2}^{15-15}{x}^{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{12}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12}{x}^{12}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{15}{12}){2}^{15-12} & =455\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =455\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =3\\,640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{12}$ is $3\\,640.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287900 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287900 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287900 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $(p,q)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $p+2q?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.36p+1.2q=0.36 \\\\ -1.9p-7.6q=3.8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afe3b13e-ab14-5340-83f7-304f398b7445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287900 #edb/ref 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equation by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.36p+1.2q=0.36 & |\\times 100 \\\\ -1.9p-7.6q=3.8 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}36p+120q=36 \\\\ -19p-76q=38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can solve for $p$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19p-76q & =38 \\\\ -19p & =76q+38 \\\\ p & =\\frac{76q+38}{-19} \\\\ p & =-4q-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $q,$ we can substitute $p=-4q-2$ into the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36p+120q & =36 \\\\ 36(-4q-2)+120q & =36 \\\\ -144q-72+120q & =36 \\\\ -24q & =108 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{108}{24} \\\\ q & =-4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $q=-4.5$ into the equation $p=-4q-2$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =-4q-2 \\\\ p & =-4(-4.5)-2 \\\\ p & =18-2 \\\\ p & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=16$ and $q=-4.5.$ So, \n\n$$\np+2q=16+2(-4.5)=16-9=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287990 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a5ebda6-bb81-57c5-aa1e-8e3aa3418e26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287990 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2-2(-3)=-2+6=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4)+3(-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-8+9=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288304 #edb/ref 1080 "p=9,\\,q=10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28be3240-f527-5881-b030-d0e2f6f588f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288324 #edb/ref 1080 "p=5,\\,q=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #e